<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Byzantine Spania</h1><p class="st-meta">552 CE – 624 CE (approx.) · capital Carthago Nova</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The East Roman foothold on Spain's south-east coast, won by Justinian in 552 and held for some seventy years against the Visigoths.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 552 the Visigothic noble <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athanagild">Athanagild</a>, rebelling against King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agila-i">Agila</a>, sought help from Justinian. An imperial force under the elderly patrician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberius-praetorian-prefect">Liberius</a> landed in the south-east, and in the following years the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> seized a coastal strip from around Cartagena to Málaga and beyond, organised as the province of Spania.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Spania was run by a magister militum based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthago-nova">Carthago Nova</a>, and an inscription of 589 records the governor Comenciolus repairing the city's defences. It formed part of Justinian's restoration of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> in the West, linked to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/praetorian-prefecture-of-africa">Byzantine Africa</a>. Its population was Hispano-Roman and Catholic, and Byzantine authority was mainly military and fiscal.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Visigothic kings steadily pushed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> back. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liuvigild">Leovigild</a> retook inland areas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisebut">Sisebut</a> captured Málaga around 615, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suintila">Suintila</a> expelled the last imperial forces around 624. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthago-nova">Carthago Nova</a> was destroyed in the fighting.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="other, 552 CE – 624 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M26 24 H74 V52 Q74 74 50 84 Q26 74 26 52 Z"/><path d="M50 24 V84 M26 46 H74"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">other</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Iberia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:70.69%;width:1.43%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>552 CE – 624 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Carthago Nova</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Liberius, Comenciolus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chalcedonian Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Spania, Provincia Spaniae</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=588&amp;focus=byzantine-spania" aria-label="Byzantine Spania on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4655 825 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Byzantine Spania lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4975 1081L5709 970M4975 1081L5035 888"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0M5035 888h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4975 1081h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4975 1081h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Carthago Nova. <a href="/atlas?year=588&amp;focus=byzantine-spania">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

